WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.870
Governing MCP for a
workforce the size of a city.

2
00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:07.922
So how many people are
using MCP in this workforce?

3
00:00:07.922 --> 00:00:09.661
Let me put it in ships.

4
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:13.115
An Enterprise's worth: four
hundred and twenty-eight?

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.949
The Enterprise-D, about a thousand?

6
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:19.017
A Battlestar's worth,
twenty-eight hundred?

7
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.825
A Halo's worth, seventeen thousand?

8
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.685
A Star Destroyer, thirty-seven thousand?

9
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:29.907
The Executor, two hundred
and eighty thousand?

10
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.964
The Death Star, one point two million?

11
00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:36.543
I am not supposed to say the exact number.

12
00:00:36.543 --> 00:00:42.981
But it is about two-thirds of a Death
Star, actively using MCP every single day.

13
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:46.690
You might think I am going
to do a technical keynote.

14
00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.854
We are at a tech conference.

15
00:00:48.854 --> 00:00:52.646
But my message today is
remarkably untechnical.

16
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:57.108
You expect the number, the
architecture, the process,

17
00:00:57.108 --> 00:01:00.830
the security issues, and the
things we thought were unusual.

18
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.786
People have covered those all
week, and it is all on the website.

19
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:08.132
What I really want you to leave with is

20
00:01:08.132 --> 00:01:10.828
the most effective lever for MCP adoption.

21
00:01:10.828 --> 00:01:13.494
It was not what I expected.

22
00:01:13.494 --> 00:01:16.574
Before that, the things you expect.

23
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:20.639
The architecture, the simple
version. A person. A device.

24
00:01:20.639 --> 00:01:24.379
An agent running on that device.

25
00:01:24.379 --> 00:01:29.500
One gate, a proxy and a registry,
that every tool call goes through.

26
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.987
And the tools behind it.

27
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:35.021
Done properly: the managed device
with its policies, tool traffic

28
00:01:35.021 --> 00:01:38.950
through the proxy and the
gateway, model traffic through

29
00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.647
its own gateway, and the
control plane feeding all of it.

30
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:46.274
Now look at the two red
dashed lines at the bottom.

31
00:01:46.274 --> 00:01:49.932
The shell and the browser never
touch any of it. Hold that thought.

32
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:53.376
Our process for accepting
a server: six gates.

33
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.796
Do we have a relationship with the vendor?

34
00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:57.594
Is there a real business need?

35
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:00.770
Is it well built, and
wrapped where it falls short?

36
00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:02.361
Does it speak the current spec?

37
00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:04.450
Does it meet our security standards?

38
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:06.263
And is the vendor certified?

39
00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:09.582
The complete checklist, with
every source, is on the site.

40
00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:14.356
One security tale that surprised us.

41
00:02:14.356 --> 00:02:19.130
CVE-2026-47250. One line in a log file.

42
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.785
An attacker plants it.

43
00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:24.373
An operator asks the agent to
read the logs, and it feeds the

44
00:02:24.373 --> 00:02:28.681
planted instruction to the agent
along with everything else.

45
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:32.247
The agent runs kubectl
against the attacker's

46
00:02:32.247 --> 00:02:34.879
server, with TLS
verification switched off.

47
00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:38.675
kubectl sends the operator's
bearer token with the request.

48
00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:42.795
The attacker now holds a token for
a cluster they could not reach.

49
00:02:42.967 --> 00:02:46.237
A hacker's game is
multi-dimensional chess:

50
00:02:46.237 --> 00:02:47.872
gather everything now, and the day that

51
00:02:47.872 --> 00:02:51.681
cluster is exposed, they
have one more piece.

52
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:54.009
And things we thought were unusual.

53
00:02:54.009 --> 00:02:58.540
We wrapped MCP servers in MCP servers,
and we thought that was weird.

54
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.773
Apparently everybody is doing it.

55
00:03:00.967 --> 00:03:03.780
The server does not meet
our security standards.

56
00:03:03.967 --> 00:03:07.113
Credentials must not cross
from client to server.

57
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.048
A hundred tools offered,
and nine presented.

58
00:03:10.233 --> 00:03:12.828
Reads and writes split
into separate servers.

59
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.797
The wrong transport.

60
00:03:14.967 --> 00:03:16.817
Or every call logged.

61
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.426
The full list, and the
tools, are on the site.

62
00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:22.037
But it is not the architecture or the

63
00:03:22.037 --> 00:03:24.007
gating process I really
want to talk about.

64
00:03:24.007 --> 00:03:28.316
What if you gave your end
users generative AI, and MCP,

65
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.095
and said:

66
00:03:29.095 --> 00:03:33.813
plug them in like USB, sprinkle
them everywhere like your favorite

67
00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:37.681
hot sauce, and relentlessly use
AI, because that is how we win?

68
00:03:37.867 --> 00:03:42.011
You handed everyone a relentless
hacker, on your hardware,

69
00:03:42.011 --> 00:03:46.328
inside your security zones, with
your AI accounts and your tools.

70
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.084
And it runs on the end-user
device, authenticated as that user.

71
00:03:51.084 --> 00:03:53.450
People forget that a laptop is a server.

72
00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:58.479
So what happens when you tell
people with superpowers no?

73
00:03:58.733 --> 00:04:01.852
Then the user made a reasonable request.

74
00:04:01.852 --> 00:04:05.120
I want a tool that reads
my mail. No problem.

75
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:09.496
Draft replies for me? Not
entirely comfortable, but fine.

76
00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:12.837
Send email as me? Whoa. Hold on.

77
00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:16.367
These are human tools
for human communication.

78
00:04:16.367 --> 00:04:21.910
A bot has to be labeled as a bot,
secured as a bot, given its own identity.

79
00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.463
Nobody has solved that yet.

80
00:04:23.633 --> 00:04:26.631
So we said no, and we
offered alternatives.

81
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.392
And the user asked the agent: I do
not see an MCP server for Outlook.

82
00:04:31.392 --> 00:04:34.087
Is there some other way we might do this?

83
00:04:34.333 --> 00:04:36.264
The agent wrote PowerShell.

84
00:04:36.264 --> 00:04:40.222
Classic Outlook has had an
automation interface for decades,

85
00:04:40.222 --> 00:04:44.295
and on a healthy, managed laptop
the script went straight through.

86
00:04:44.295 --> 00:04:46.427
It read and sent mail as the user.

87
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:50.408
Then they asked about
Teams. We said no again.

88
00:04:50.408 --> 00:04:54.554
So they put the approved browser
in remote debugging mode,

89
00:04:54.554 --> 00:04:59.552
and the agent walked the browser,
and the browser walked Teams.

90
00:04:59.552 --> 00:05:02.573
It never opened Teams.
It opened a browser.

91
00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:05.281
Remember the two red dashed lines?

92
00:05:05.281 --> 00:05:08.140
The shell and the browser
never touch the gate.

93
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.680
So what was the biggest lever?
The policy? The architecture?

94
00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:16.227
The proxies, the registries?
None of that alone.

95
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:21.436
We had to win the hearts and minds of
users, because for the first time an

96
00:05:21.436 --> 00:05:26.418
internal team is a SaaS company, and
its biggest threat is its own end users.

97
00:05:26.418 --> 00:05:30.561
If you do not get their buy-in,
they ask the agent to do it.

98
00:05:30.561 --> 00:05:32.801
That is how you get shadow IT.

99
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.736
So love your users. Figure out what
they need, and get them to yes.

100
00:05:37.933 --> 00:05:41.119
Say no when you must, and explain why.

101
00:05:41.119 --> 00:05:44.817
A no without a why is a
workaround waiting to happen.

102
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.177
Send the signals:

103
00:05:46.177 --> 00:05:50.581
let them vote, let them ask, let
them tell you where the openings are.

104
00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.847
And run the service like a product:

105
00:05:52.847 --> 00:05:56.817
a self-service form, a
feedback path, a leaderboard,

106
00:05:56.817 --> 00:05:59.884
communities of practice,
done relentlessly.

107
00:06:00.133 --> 00:06:01.608
Talk to your users.

108
00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.805
The architecture, the policies,
the process: yes, all of that.

109
00:06:05.805 --> 00:06:08.739
But the biggest lever is a relationship

110
00:06:08.739 --> 00:06:11.793
with the users who
consume your MCP servers.

111
00:06:11.967 --> 00:06:15.655
Talk and listen. It is not
just a technology problem.

112
00:06:15.655 --> 00:06:19.588
More than ever it is a human problem,
and humans can help us solve it.

113
00:06:19.767 --> 00:06:24.491
The details are at mcp dot
michael rishi forrester dot com.

114
00:06:24.491 --> 00:06:25.322
Come find me.

